The UI pattern: GitHub-style modal with tabs.

Click project → modal opens → navigate tabs.

The tabs:

1. Overview
- Project info cards
- Health metrics
- Quick stats

2. Tasks & Progress
- Real TODO.md loaded from file
- Markdown parsed and rendered
- Checkboxes styled

3. Files & Context
- Project file browser
- CLAUDE.md rules displayed
- Key files highlighted

4. Settings
- Port assignments
- Configuration options
- Project metadata

The implementation:

HTML structure:

<div class="modal">
  <div class="tabs">
    <button data-tab="overview">Overview</button>
    <button data-tab="tasks">Tasks</button>
    <button data-tab="files">Files</button>
  </div>
  <div class="tab-content" id="tab-content">
    <!-- Content loads here -->
  </div>
</div>

JavaScript:

tabs.forEach(tab => {
  tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
    showTabContent(tab.dataset.tab, currentProject);
  });
});

async function showTabContent(tabName, project) {
  switch(tabName) {
    case 'tasks':
      const todo = await loadProjectTODO(project);
      tabContent.innerHTML = markdownToHtml(todo);
      break;
    // ... other tabs
  }
}

The file loading:

async function loadProjectTODO(project) {
  // Tries TODO.md, todo.md, tasks.md
  for (const filename of ['TODO.md', 'todo.md', 'tasks.md']) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(
        `http://localhost:8080/read-file?path=${project.path}/${filename}`
      );
      if (response.ok) return await response.text();
    } catch (e) { continue; }
  }
  throw new Error('No TODO file found');
}

Takeaway: Good dashboards load real data. Tabs organize complexity. File servers enable secure access.

